// en-safety — generated from src/en-safety-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Construction Safety / HSE Manager. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["en-safety"] = (QB["en-safety"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does a construction safety manager do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I protect everyone on site from harm — developing and enforcing the safety program, conducting inspections and audits, training workers, investigating incidents, ensuring OSHA compliance, and building a culture where safety is genuinely valued, not just posted on a wall. It's part technical (hazard control), part compliance, and largely about influencing behavior and culture. The measure of success is that everyone goes home safe, every day.\"\n\nTip: Prevent harm through program, compliance and culture.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Is safety more about rules or culture?","Both, but culture wins — rules without a culture where people genuinely care and speak up don't prevent incidents."]}],
["Regulations", "What OSHA standards are most relevant to construction?", "**Why they ask:** compliance core.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"OSHA 29 CFR 1926 is the construction standard. The highest-risk areas map to the 'fatal four': fall protection (the most-cited standard), electrical safety, struck-by hazards, and caught-in/between like trenching and excavation. Plus scaffolding, ladders, PPE, hazard communication, and confined spaces. I focus my program on the hazards that actually kill and injure people, not just paperwork.\"\n\nTip: 1926 standard; focus on the fatal four.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What are the fatal four?","Falls, electrocution, struck-by, and caught-in/between — the leading causes of construction deaths."]}],
["Fall protection", "How do you manage fall protection?", "**Why they ask:** the #1 construction hazard.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Falls are the leading cause of construction deaths, so I take them seriously. Generally fall protection is required at six feet — through guardrails, safety nets, or personal fall arrest systems with proper anchorage. I ensure the right system for each task, that workers are trained and using it correctly, equipment is inspected, and there's a rescue plan for anyone who falls while arrested. Enforcement here is strict and non-negotiable.\"\n\nTip: Six-foot rule; right system, training, anchorage, rescue plan.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why do you need a rescue plan for fall arrest?","Hanging in a harness causes suspension trauma within minutes — you must be able to rescue a fallen worker fast."]}],
["Hazard analysis", "What is a Job Hazard Analysis?", "**Why they ask:** a core prevention tool.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A JHA (or JSA) breaks a task into steps, identifies the hazards at each step, and defines controls to eliminate or reduce them — done before the work, with the crew involved. It's proactive: find and control the hazard before someone gets hurt. Involving the workers who do the task makes it realistic and builds their ownership. I use JHAs especially for high-risk or non-routine work.\"\n\nTip: Steps → hazards → controls, done with the crew, before the work.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the hierarchy of controls?","Elimination, substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls, then PPE — most to least effective."]}],
["Investigation", "How do you investigate an incident?", "**Why they ask:** learning from incidents.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Secure the scene and care for the injured first, then gather facts — interviews, photos, conditions — and find the root cause, not just the immediate one. The goal is prevention, not blame; 'worker was careless' is never a real root cause. I look at what in the system allowed it — training, procedure, equipment, pressure — and put in corrective actions, then verify they work. Near-misses get investigated too, because they're free lessons.\"\n\nTip: Root cause, not blame; investigate near-misses too.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why isn't 'worker was careless' a root cause?","It stops you from fixing the real system issue — why was the hazard there, and why was unsafe behavior possible or incentivized?"]}],
["Culture", "How do you build a strong safety culture?", "**Why they ask:** the real driver of safety.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Visible leadership commitment, involving workers (safety committees, JHAs, their input), recognizing safe behavior, responding to concerns without blame so people report hazards and near-misses, and consistency — the rules apply to everyone including management. When workers believe the company genuinely cares and they can stop unsafe work without fear, incidents drop. Culture beats enforcement alone.\"\n\nTip: Leadership, worker involvement, no-blame reporting, consistency.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a sign of weak safety culture?","Underreporting of near-misses, production pressure overriding safety, and workers afraid to stop unsafe work."]}],
["Metrics", "What safety metrics do you track?", "**Why they ask:** measuring performance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Lagging indicators like TRIR (total recordable incident rate), DART rate, and lost-time injuries, plus leading indicators like near-miss reports, inspections completed, training rates, and hazard corrections. I value leading indicators because they predict and prevent, whereas lagging ones only count harm after it happened. A rise in near-miss reporting can actually be good — it means people are engaged.\"\n\nTip: Leading indicators predict; lagging ones count after harm.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What is TRIR?","Total Recordable Incident Rate — recordable injuries per 100 full-time workers per year, a standard safety benchmark."]}],
["Training", "How do you make safety training effective?", "**Why they ask:** training drives behavior.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Make it relevant and practical to the actual hazards and tasks, hands-on rather than just slides, in the workers' language, and reinforced through toolbox talks and on-the-job coaching, not one-and-done. People retain what they practice and what connects to their real work. I also verify competency, because sitting through training isn't the same as being able to do it safely.\"\n\nTip: Relevant, hands-on, reinforced; verify competency.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's a toolbox talk?","A short, focused safety discussion with the crew before work, usually on a specific hazard for that day's tasks."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time you stopped unsafe work or prevented an incident.", "**Why they ask:** real experience and courage.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I saw a crew about to enter a trench that wasn't properly shored or sloped — a cave-in risk that kills quickly. I stopped the work immediately, explained why, and we installed proper protection before continuing. There was schedule pressure, but a trench collapse is fatal and non-negotiable. The crew understood, and it reinforced that stopping unsafe work is expected, not punished.\"\n\nTip: Show you stop unsafe work firmly despite pressure.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why are trenches so dangerous?","A cave-in can bury and kill a worker in seconds — a cubic yard of soil weighs over a ton. Protection is mandatory."]}],
["Leadership", "How do you handle pushback from a superintendent under schedule pressure?", "**Why they ask:** influence and firmness.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I partner rather than police — understand their pressure and find a way to do the work safely and efficiently, since safety and productivity usually align. But on a serious hazard, I hold firm and explain the real consequences: an injury, a fatality, an OSHA shutdown, cost everyone far more than the time saved. I bring data and solutions, and I have leadership backing that safety isn't negotiable.\"\n\nTip: Partner and solve, but hold firm on serious hazards.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why do safety and productivity usually align?","Incidents cause shutdowns, rework, investigations and morale hits — a safe, well-run site is also an efficient one."]}],
["Compliance", "How do you prepare for an OSHA inspection?", "**Why they ask:** compliance readiness.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Stay continuously ready — the best preparation is a genuinely safe site every day. I keep required records (training, inspections, the OSHA 300 log), ensure programs are documented and followed, do regular self-audits to find and fix issues first, and make sure supervisors know their roles. During an inspection I'm cooperative, factual and professional. Readiness comes from doing safety right daily, not scrambling when OSHA arrives.\"\n\nTip: Continuous readiness beats last-minute prep; keep records current.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the OSHA 300 log?","The required record of work-related injuries and illnesses that employers must maintain and post annually."]}],
["Closing", "Why construction safety?", "**Why they ask:** motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Because it genuinely saves lives — I help people go home to their families safely, which is deeply meaningful. It combines technical hazard knowledge, compliance, and the people side of changing behavior and culture, and the stakes make it matter. I take real pride in a site where everyone stays safe.\"\n\nTip: Genuine, life-saving purpose plus technical and people work.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What certification do safety managers pursue?","OSHA 30, and professional certs like the CHST or CSP for career growth."]}]
]);

